# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
# Revisions: Section 5 (Theoretical Framework), part B: 5.5-5.13

REVS = {
    # 5.5
    "P203": ("An energy system is usually described as a chain:",
             "An energy system is usually described as a chain: generation, transmission, distribution, metering, billing, appliances and end use. The description is accurate as far as it goes, but it separates the technical from the social and then treats the social as the place where the technical is received. A posthumanist account describes the same system as an assemblage: a configuration of people, materials, infrastructures, institutions, fuels, dwellings, meters, tariffs and practices whose relations produce the capacity to deliver an energy service."),
    "P204": ("The difference is not merely semantic.",
             "The difference is not merely semantic. In the chain description, the household is the end point. In the assemblage description, the household is one element among many, and the service is produced by the relations between them: a cooking service is produced by the stove, the fuel, the pot, the kitchen, the person who cooks, the money that buys the fuel, the tariff that sets the price, the reliability of the supply and the norms that govern what counts as a proper meal. Change any one of these and the service changes."),
    "P205": ("This way of thinking has become more common in energy social science.",
             "This way of thinking has become more common in energy social science. Sovacool (2014), reviewing fifteen years of energy scholarship, calls for an agenda that takes infrastructure, practice and politics seriously, and the multi-level perspective in transition studies describes how niches, regimes and landscapes interact over time (Geels, 2002, 2011). That literature explains why transitions are slow and why a promising niche can fail to displace an incumbent regime, but it operates at a high level of aggregation: it can explain the fate of a technology category, and it is less good at explaining why one digester works and the one next door does not."),
    "P206": ("The value of posthumanism is that it operates at the scale",
             "The value of posthumanism is that it operates at the scale of the configuration without losing the wider structures. It can hold the micro and the macro in one frame, because both are composed of relations: the tariff, the municipal by-law, the landlord's rule, the stove and the pot are all elements of the same assemblage, even though they operate at different scales."),

    # 5.5.1 worked illustration: compressed from 12 paragraphs to 6
    "P208": ("Consider a community-scale biogas digester proposed for an informal settlement.",
             "Consider a community-scale biogas digester proposed for an informal settlement. A conventional engineering assessment would specify the feedstock, the hydraulic retention time, the digester volume, the gas yield and the capital cost. A TAM study would add measures of perceived usefulness and ease of use, and perhaps a question about whether residents would recommend the system to a neighbour. Both would produce useful information. Neither would explain what happens next. A posthumanist analysis asks a different set of questions."),
    "P209": ("A posthumanist analysis asks a different set of questions.",
             "Feedstock and water. Who produces the organic waste, who collects it, and what else do they do with their time? If the waste is currently sold or fed to animals, the digester is competing with an existing use. If collection is done by women or children, the labour cost falls on them. If the supply is seasonal, the digester's output will be seasonal. Digestion also requires water in a fixed ratio to feedstock, and in a settlement where water is shared, purchased or carried, the digester competes with cooking, cleaning and drinking. A design that assumes a tap in the yard has already excluded the households that are most in need. The feedstock is not an input to the technology; it is a relation between the technology, the household economy and the division of labour."),
    "P210": ("Feedstock. Who produces the organic waste,",
             "Space, temperature and cooking. A digester needs a footprint, a slope, access for maintenance and a safe distance from dwellings, and in a dense settlement space is not empty: it is claimed, used and contested, so the siting decision is a decision about whose space is taken and who is compensated. Microbial activity depends on temperature, so a system that performs well in a warm climate may underperform in winter or at altitude \u2014 a material property that shapes the service that can be promised, not a detail to be handled by a safety factor. And gas burns differently from wood or paraffin: the flame is less visible, the heat is more concentrated, and the pot may need to be different. A technology that ignores the pot and the dish will be judged unusable by the person who cooks, whatever the gas yield."),
    "P211": ("Water. Digestion requires water,",
             "Maintenance, labour and institutions. A digester needs someone to check the feed, remove the digestate, clear a blockage and replace a valve \u2014 someone with time, tools, training and a reason to keep going. If the arrangement depends on a volunteer who moves away, the technology has a design fault, not a social problem. The labour of feeding, cleaning and maintaining a household energy system is usually assigned to women, so a system that increases that labour while reducing smoke may be a good intervention on one dimension and a bad one on another; the posthumanist frame makes this distribution visible because it treats labour as part of the assemblage rather than as a preference. And the digester may require a permit, a waste collection agreement, a municipal connection or a credit arrangement. These are not \u201cenabling environment\u201d factors that sit outside the project; they are elements whose presence or absence changes what the technology is."),
    "P212": ("Space. A digester needs a footprint,",
             "None of this is invisible to a good engineer. Experienced practitioners know that feedstock, water, space and maintenance decide whether a system survives. The contribution of the theory is not the list of factors. It is the claim that the factors are constitutive of the technology rather than external to it, and that the unit of design is therefore the configuration. If that claim is accepted, the design process changes. It must prototype the arrangement, not only the artefact: the meter, the tariff, the dwelling, the maintenance contract and the waste supply belong in the prototype, and it is the arrangement, not only the device, that must be evaluated."),
    "P213": ("Temperature. Microbial activity depends on temperature.",
             "A TAM study would report that residents find the idea of biogas useful. A posthumanist study would report that the system will fail in the households where water is carried, succeed in the households where a market provides a steady waste stream, and produce a new burden for the women who are expected to feed it. The second account is less tidy, but it is more useful for design."),
    "P214": ("Cooking. Gas burns differently from wood or paraffin.", None),  # delete (merged)
    "P215": ("Maintenance. A digester needs someone to check the feed,", None),
    "P216": ("Gender. The labour of feeding, cleaning and maintaining", None),
    "P217": ("Institutions. The digester may require a permit,", None),
    "P218": ("None of this is invisible to a good engineer.", None),
    "P219": ("The TAM study would tell us that residents find the idea of biogas useful.", None),

    # 5.6
    "P221": ("The normative core of this study is not posthumanism alone.",
             "The normative core of this study is not posthumanism alone. It is a conversation between posthumanism and energy justice, in which each supplies something the other lacks."),
    "P222": ("Posthumanism adds four things to the justice literature.",
             "Posthumanism adds four things to the justice literature. First, it extends the moral community beyond humans: materials, ecologies and future generations are not merely resources to be allocated but participants in the arrangement whose flourishing matters. Second, it reframes justice as a property of composition rather than only of distribution: the question is not only how much of a benefit each household receives, but which actors and materials are admitted to the arrangement in the first place. Third, it makes visible forms of exclusion that a human-centred recognition analysis can miss: a dwelling that cannot accommodate a technology, or an infrastructure that excludes by design, is a form of injustice that does not appear in a survey of attitudes. Fourth, it supplies a critique of the technocratic view from nowhere: the designer who claims to be neutral is usually occupying a position that others do not have."),
    "P223": ("Posthumanism also has limits, and they should be stated plainly.",
             "Posthumanism also has limits, and they should be stated plainly. It does not specify distributive criteria, so it cannot tell a municipality how to allocate a budget between two settlements. It does not generate policy instruments, it has no theory of institutions, and it can be vague about how decisions are actually made. Applied without a human-centred justice vocabulary, it can obscure human suffering by dissolving it into ontology. A woman who cannot afford paraffin is not a \u201cphenomenon\u201d. She is a person in a specific relation of deprivation, and the analysis must be able to say so."),
    "P224": ("Energy justice supplies the missing operational content.",
             "Energy justice supplies the missing operational content. The four dimensions used in this study \u2014 distribution, procedure, recognition and restoration \u2014 provide criteria that can be assessed: distribution asks who receives the service and who bears the cost; procedure asks who participates and on what terms; recognition asks whose knowledge and practices count; and restoration asks what past harm is repaired and what future capacity is built. These are not derived from posthumanism. They are derived from the justice literature (McCauley et al., 2013; Jenkins et al., 2016; Heffron and McCauley, 2017), and they are retained as a normative-operational complement."),
    "P225": ("Justice also has a scalar dimension.",
             "Justice also has a scalar dimension. Fraser (2009) argues that the question of the frame \u2014 who is included in the \u201cwho\u201d of justice \u2014 is itself a question of justice. In an energy project, the frame may be the household, the street, the settlement, the municipality or the planet, and each frame makes some claims visible and others invisible. A project that is just at the scale of the settlement may be unjust at the scale of the region if it displaces pollution or extracts water. The study does not resolve this problem, but it names it and builds a scalar check into the evaluation."),
    "P226": ("The combination is therefore deliberate.",
             "The combination is therefore deliberate. Posthumanism provides the ontology and the critique of technocracy; energy justice provides the criteria and the policy vocabulary; systems thinking provides the tools for mapping relations and feedback. None of the three is sufficient alone, and the framework is designed so that each can be held accountable by the others."),
    "P227": ("One further point is necessary.",
             "One further point is necessary. The literature on just transition has been criticised for treating \u201cthe community\u201d as a homogeneous beneficiary, and the same criticism applies to a posthumanist framework if it is not careful. A settlement is stratified by tenure, income, gender, age, ethnicity and political affiliation, and a project that benefits \u201cthe community\u201d may benefit one group within it and burden another. The framework therefore requires disaggregated evidence, and it does not assume that a community-level outcome is a household-level outcome."),

    # 5.7
    "P230": ("User-centred design begins from the needs and experience of the user.",
             "User-centred design begins from the needs and experience of the user. Participatory design goes further, involving users in the design process, and co-creation and co-research extend participation to the generation of ideas and the production of knowledge (Sanders and Stappers, 2008; von Hippel, 2005). These traditions have improved design practice and given users a voice they did not have before."),
    "P231": ("They also carry humanist assumptions.",
             "They also carry humanist assumptions. The user is treated as a centre of intention and experience, the artefact as a tool the user employs, and participation as a process of extracting knowledge from users and transferring it into a design. The designer remains the one who decides what counts as knowledge and what counts as a good design: the user is consulted, but the frame of the consultation is set elsewhere."),
    "P232": ("These assumptions are not wrong in every context.",
             "These assumptions are not wrong in every context, but they are inadequate for a technology whose performance depends on the arrangement around it. If the problem is the water supply, the tenure arrangement or the maintenance contract, then consulting users about their preferences for a stove will not solve it. Participation has to extend to the elements that the design tradition treats as context."),
    "P234": ("A posthumanist reconstruction treats the end-user",
             "A posthumanist reconstruction treats the end-user as a position within an assemblage rather than as a centre of intention. The end-user is the person whose practices, body, dwelling and relationships are enrolled in the technology. Their agency is real, but it is not the only agency at work: the artefact, the fuel, the infrastructure and the institution also act, and the outcome is produced by the relation. This does not diminish the user; it makes the user's knowledge more important, not less. If the outcome depends on the relation, then the person who lives in the relation has access to knowledge that the designer does not \u2014 knowledge that is situated, embodied and practical, not always articulate and not always consistent, but indispensable."),
    "P235": ("This does not diminish the user.", None),
    "P237": ("The literature often asserts that users hold valuable knowledge",
             "The literature often asserts that users hold valuable knowledge without saying what kind. Five types are relevant here."),
    "P238": ("Tacit knowledge is knowledge that is embedded in practice",
             "Tacit knowledge is embedded in practice and difficult to articulate: a cook knows when a pot is at the right temperature by sound and feel, not by a thermometer, and a household knows how much paraffin to buy in a given week without calculating it. Situated knowledge is produced from a particular position (Haraway, 1988): a tenant knows the landlord's rules in a way an owner does not, and a woman knows the risks of cooking in a shared kitchen in a way a male researcher may not. Embodied knowledge is held in the body \u2014 the physical effort of carrying water, the heat of a stove, the smoke in the eyes, the weight of a pot \u2014 and cannot be reduced to information (Hayles, 1999)."),
    "P239": ("Situated knowledge is knowledge produced from a particular position", None),
    "P240": ("Embodied knowledge is knowledge held in the body.", None),
    "P241": ("Practice-based knowledge is knowledge of how a task is actually done,",
             "Practice-based knowledge is knowledge of how a task is actually done, as distinct from how it is supposed to be done: workarounds, repairs, substitutions and the informal rules that make a system work. Cultural knowledge is knowledge of what a technology means: the appropriateness of a fuel for a dish, the symbolism of a flame, the social meaning of cooking for guests and the norms that govern who cooks and when. A design process that recognises these types of knowledge will use different methods to elicit them: interviews for articulate knowledge; observation and transects for tacit and practice-based knowledge; diaries and photovoice for embodied and situated knowledge; and workshops for cultural knowledge, if they are organised in a way that allows disagreement."),
    "P242": ("Cultural knowledge is knowledge of what a technology means.", None),
    "P243": ("A design process that recognises these types of knowledge", None),

    # 5.7.4
    "P245": ("The earlier draft of this proposal treated culture as a barrier to acceptance.",
             "Treating culture as a barrier to acceptance is misleading, and this study does not do so. A cultural practice is not an obstacle to a technology; it is part of the standard against which the technology is judged. If a dish requires a visible flame, a technology that provides an invisible flame is not culturally neutral and technically adequate; it is inadequate for that dish. The question is not how to overcome the culture but how to design a technology that can be enrolled in it, or how to negotiate a change in practice that the household considers acceptable."),
    "P246": ("This does not mean that all practices should be accepted uncritically.",
             "This does not mean that all practices should be accepted uncritically. Some practices are harmful, and some are constrained by poverty rather than by preference. The analysis must therefore distinguish between a preference, a constraint and a compromise: a household that uses paraffin because it cannot afford electricity is managing a constraint, not expressing a cultural preference, while a household that uses wood because the food tastes better is expressing a preference. The design response is different in each case."),

    # 5.7.5
    "P248": ("Participation is not automatically good.",
             "Participation is not automatically good, and a framework that ignores its failure modes will romanticise the community it studies. Tokenism means residents are invited but not heard, their presence legitimising a decision already made (Arnstein, 1969). Elite capture means those with status, education or political connections dominate the process and direct the benefits towards themselves. Expectation inflation means the project promises more than it can deliver, and the disappointment damages trust in future projects."),
    "P249": ("Tokenism. Residents are invited but not heard.", None),
    "P250": ("Elite capture. Those with status, education or political connections",
             "Consultation fatigue arises when communities are asked to participate repeatedly in projects that do not endure, and the cost of participation falls on those with the least time. Framing power means the agenda, the language and the technical vocabulary are set by the project, so residents can respond within the frame but cannot change it. The labour of participation is often invisible: it is counted in meetings attended, not in the time and effort contributed, and it is often borne by women. And exit means the project ends, the funding stops, and the arrangements built around it collapse \u2014 participation without a sustainability plan is a temporary relationship. A posthumanist analysis adds a further failure mode: material exclusion. A resident may be willing to participate but unable to attend because the meeting is held at a time when she is cooking, in a place she cannot reach, or in a language she does not speak comfortably. The barrier is not attitudinal; it is material and institutional, and it can be designed against."),
    "P251": ("Expectation inflation.", None),
    "P252": ("Consultation fatigue.", None),
    "P253": ("Framing power.", None),
    "P254": ("Invisibility of labour.", None),
    "P255": ("Exit.", None),
    "P256": ("A posthumanist analysis adds a further failure mode: material exclusion.", None),

    # 5.7.6
    "P258": ("The study replaces the attitudinal construct of acceptance",
             "The study replaces the attitudinal construct of acceptance with a relational one. Acceptance is a pattern of sustained use that emerges when technology, infrastructure, household practice, cost and social norms align. It is not a decision made once; it is an achievement that has to be reproduced every day."),
    "P259": ("This definition has four implications.",
             "The definition has four implications. First, acceptance is observable in practice, not only in stated intention: a household that says it likes the system but does not use it has not accepted it. Second, acceptance is provisional: it can be lost when a part fails, a price rises or a relationship changes. Third, acceptance is distributed: it depends on the maintenance arrangement, the fuel supply and the institution as much as on the household. Fourth, acceptance is not the same as justice: a technology can be accepted because the household has no alternative, and that acceptance can coexist with exploitation."),
    "P260": ("The definition also makes acceptance measurable",
             "The definition also makes acceptance measurable in a way that is useful for design: the study can observe use over time, record the points at which use declines, and trace the relations that changed. That is more demanding than a satisfaction survey, but it produces evidence that can be acted on."),

    # 5.8
    "P262": ("A Living Lab is a real-world arrangement for co-creation.",
             "Section 4.4 introduced the Living Lab as a real-world arrangement for co-creation, distinguished by who drives it and by whether it is primarily a space, a method or a network (Bergvall-K\u00e5reborn and St\u00e5hlbr\u00f6st, 2009; Leminen et al., 2012; Mbatha and Musango, 2022). What concerns this section is what would make such a lab systemic rather than systemic only in name."),
    "P263": ("Systems thinking adds three resources.",
             "Systems thinking adds three resources. The first is boundary critique: Checkland (1981) argues that the boundary of a system is a choice, and Midgley (2000) shows that the choice has political consequences, because who is inside the system and who is in the environment determines whose interests are considered. The second is feedback: Meadows (2008) shows that the behaviour of a system is produced by loops, delays and stocks, not by the intentions of its members. The third is emergence: Cilliers (1998) argues that complex systems have properties that cannot be predicted from their components, and that any model is a reduction."),
    "P264": ("A Living Lab becomes systemic, in this study, when three things are true.",
             "A Living Lab becomes systemic, in this study, when three things are true. First, its boundary is chosen through an explicit critique rather than by convention: the lab does not assume that the project, the settlement or the municipality is the relevant system, but asks which boundary would include the relations that matter, and records what is excluded. Second, its feedback architecture is designed: the lab collects data on the effects of its own interventions and uses them to revise the intervention. Third, its evaluation criteria are derived from the system's purpose, not from the project's outputs: it asks whether the arrangement became more just and more secure, not only whether the prototype worked."),
    "P265": ("There is a further, distinctly posthumanist point.",
             "There is a further, distinctly posthumanist point. A Living Lab is an apparatus in Barad's sense: it does not observe a phenomenon from outside; it participates in producing the phenomenon. The questions it asks, the instruments it uses, the people it includes and the data it records all enact a cut, which makes some things visible and others invisible. Reflexivity about the cut is therefore a condition of validity, not an optional virtue: a lab that presents its findings as neutral observations has misunderstood its own role."),
    "P266": ("This has practical consequences.",
             "This has practical consequences. The lab must document its own decisions \u2014 why this site, why these participants, why this technology, why these indicators. Dissenting views must be recorded, not smoothed into consensus. The lab must be accountable to the people it studies, not only to the funder. And the evaluation must ask what the lab itself changed, including changes that were not intended."),
    "P267": ("The study derives six design principles for a systemic Living Lab",
             "From this argument the study derives six design principles for a systemic Living Lab. They are stated here and operationalised in Section 6."),
    "P268": ("Boundary transparency.", "Boundary transparency: the lab states whose interests are included and excluded, and revisits the boundary as the project develops."),
    "P269": ("Distributed participation.", "Distributed participation: participation is designed around the material conditions of residents' lives, including time, location, language, care responsibilities and safety."),
    "P270": ("Configuration as prototype.", "Configuration as prototype: the lab prototypes the arrangement, including the artefact, the fuel supply, the maintenance arrangement, the tariff and the governance rules."),
    "P271": ("Reflexive apparatus.", "Reflexive apparatus: the lab records its own interventions and treats them as part of the data."),
    "P272": ("Justice as an outcome and a process.", "Justice as an outcome and a process: the lab evaluates distribution, procedure, recognition and restoration, and applies the same criteria to its own operation."),
    "P273": ("Exit and durability.", "Exit and durability: the lab plans for its own withdrawal from the start, including who will own, maintain and finance the arrangement after the project ends."),
    "P274": ("These principles are not a recipe.",
             "These principles are not a recipe. They are commitments that shape how the lab is organised, and they can be assessed."),

    # 5.9
    "P276": ("The theoretical framework is not posthumanism alone.",
             "The theoretical framework is not posthumanism alone. It uses three supporting perspectives, and it explicitly rejects one. Table 3.3 sets out the adjudication."),
    "P279": ("The rejection of TAM requires justification,",
             "The rejection of TAM requires justification, because TAM is the default theory in much applied energy and engineering research. The reason is not that TAM is unfashionable or that it has been superseded. It is that its ontological assumptions are incompatible with the assumptions of this study, and using both without reconciliation would produce incoherent findings. Table 3.4 sets out the conflict."),
    "P280": ("Table 3.4 sets out the conflict.", None),
    "P283": ("TAM can still be used in two limited ways.",
             "TAM can still be used in two limited ways. It can be used as a contrast, to show what an individualist account would conclude and what it would miss. And it can supply a practitioner vocabulary for discussing perceived usefulness and ease of use, provided that those terms are not treated as explanations. What it cannot do is serve as the framework that organises the study."),

    # 5.10
    "P285": ("The theory is load-bearing for every question.",
             "The theory is load-bearing for every question. Table 3.5 sets out the relationship, and two examples show what it means in practice."),
    "P288": ("For RQ1, the theory does more than provide a definition",
             "For RQ1, the theory supplies an account of what a system is, who decides its boundary and how inequality is reproduced through the relations that the boundary includes or excludes. Without that account, the question can be answered by a taxonomy of Living Lab types, which is descriptive but not explanatory. For RQ2, the theory explains why participation is not simply a matter of inviting people: participation is an effect of the relations that make it possible \u2014 time, transport, language, care responsibilities, tenure, trust and the distribution of authority \u2014 and the theory directs attention to the material arrangements that sustain or undermine those relations."),
    "P289": ("For RQ2, the theory explains why participation", None),
    "P290": ("For RQ3, the theory explains why acceptance cannot be measured",
             "For RQ3, the theory explains why acceptance cannot be measured by asking people whether they like a technology: acceptance is produced in the relation between the artefact, the fuel, the dwelling, the practice and the institution, so the study observes use over time and traces the points at which it changes. For RQ4, it explains why a mechanism that works in one configuration may fail in another, directing the design towards leverage points \u2014 the maintenance arrangement, the fuel supply, the tariff, the governance rule and the siting decision \u2014 and insisting that a mechanism is not just unless it can be shown to distribute benefits and burdens fairly. For RQ5, it provides the criteria for evaluation: the lab is assessed not only on whether it produced a working prototype but on whether it changed the configuration in a way that increased justice and security. That is a higher bar, and it is the bar that the study sets."),
    "P291": ("For RQ4, the theory explains why a mechanism", None),
    "P292": ("For RQ5, the theory provides the criteria for evaluation.", None),

    # 5.11
    "P294": ("Six criteria are used to select the theory.",
             "Six criteria are used to select the theory: explanatory power, ontological fit, capacity to handle human and non-human elements, normative adequacy, empirical tractability and institutional fit."),
    "P295": ("Explanatory power. Posthumanism explains why identical technologies",
             "Explanatory power. Posthumanism explains why identical technologies produce different outcomes in different households and streets, why participation can fail even when it is well organised, and why justice is a property of the arrangement rather than an add-on."),
    "P296": ("Ontological fit. The research questions are about relations,",
             "Ontological fit. The research questions are about relations, not about individuals or variables, and posthumanism is the only candidate that takes the relation as its primary unit: TAM takes the individual, diffusion theory the population, practice theory the practice. Each captures something, but none captures the full configuration."),
    "P297": ("Human and non-human elements. The study has to hold people,",
             "Human and non-human elements. The study has to hold people, artefacts, infrastructures, fuels, institutions and ecologies in one frame, and posthumanism does this without reducing one to the other. Systems thinking can also do it, but it tends to treat the elements as components of a functional whole rather than as participants with unequal power."),
    "P298": ("Normative adequacy. The study is about justice.",
             "Normative adequacy. The study is about justice, and posthumanism supplies a normative vocabulary that is not reducible to efficiency, complemented by energy justice, which supplies the criteria; the combination is stronger than either alone."),
    "P299": ("Empirical tractability. Posthumanism is often criticised for being abstract.",
             "Empirical tractability. Posthumanism is often criticised for being abstract, and this study addresses the criticism by operationalising the concepts as observable relations: intra-action becomes the relation between the artefact, the household and the infrastructure; distributed agency becomes the set of actors that must be enrolled for the system to work; situated knowledge becomes the knowledge held by residents and elicited through specific methods. The test of the framework is whether it can be used in the field, not whether it is elegant."),
    "P300": ("Institutional fit. The theory was introduced by the supervisor",
             "Institutional fit. The theory was introduced by the supervisor and is supported by the research programme in which the study is located. That is a reason to take it seriously, but it is not a sufficient reason to adopt it; the argument of this section is that the theory is defensible on its merits. If it were not, the institutional fit would be a reason to negotiate rather than a reason to proceed."),
    "P301": ("Several alternatives were considered.",
             "Several alternatives were considered."),
    "P302": ("TAM and UTAUT were rejected for the reasons set out in Section 5.9.",
             "TAM and UTAUT were rejected for the reasons set out in Section 5.9: they are useful for measuring attitudes but not for explaining configurations. Diffusion of innovations explains how an innovation spreads through a population, which is useful for understanding adoption curves and the role of early adopters, but it is less useful for understanding why a technology that has spread can be abandoned, or why the benefits of diffusion are unevenly distributed."),
    "P303": ("Diffusion of innovations explains how an innovation spreads", None),
    "P304": ("Social practice theory is close to this study",
             "Social practice theory is close to this study in its attention to routine, materiality and convention (Shove, 2003; Stephenson et al., 2010), and it informs the analysis of cooking and fuel stacking; its limitation is that it is stronger on reproduction than on intervention, explaining why practices persist but saying less about how a design team might change them. Actor-network theory is the closest alternative: it shares the commitment to symmetry between humans and non-humans and provides a rich empirical vocabulary for tracing associations, but it is deliberately indifferent to normative questions, and this study needs a theory that can say something about justice, not only about association."),
    "P305": ("Actor-network theory is the closest alternative.", None),
    "P306": ("The capability approach (Nussbaum, 2011) provides a normative framework",
             "The capability approach (Nussbaum, 2011) provides a normative framework for evaluating wellbeing and freedom. It is compatible with the justice dimension of this study and could be used to specify what people are able to do, but it does not, by itself, provide an account of technology or of material agency, so it is a possible complement rather than a foundation. Political ecology and institutional theory explain the political and organisational conditions of energy transitions and inform the analysis of municipal capacity and vested interests, but they operate at a different level from the present study, which is concerned with the design of a local arrangement. Systems thinking alone was rejected because it can describe a configuration without asking whether the configuration is just; it is retained as a methodological complement, not as the foundation."),
    "P307": ("Political ecology and institutional theory", None),
    "P308": ("Systems thinking alone was rejected", None),
    "P309": ("The verdict is that posthumanism is retained",
             "The verdict is that posthumanism is retained as the primary foundation because it is the only candidate that supplies an ontology adequate to the human\u2013technology\u2013infrastructure relation at the heart of the study. It is not sufficient on its own: systems thinking supplies the tools, energy justice the criteria, Living Lab theory the method, and socio-technical theory the wider transition context."),
    "P310": ("The theory also has limits that should be stated.",
             "The theory also has limits that should be stated. It explains individual motivation poorly, it does not produce measurable acceptance scores, it offers limited guidance on policy design, and it can be difficult to write clearly. These limits are real, and the study manages them by using mixed qualitative methods and by keeping the empirical claims modest."),

    # 5.12
    "P312": ("The framework can be stated as six propositions.",
             "The framework can be stated as six propositions. They are not hypotheses in the statistical sense; they are claims that the empirical work will examine, qualify and, where necessary, revise."),
    "P313": ("P1. Acceptance is an emergent property",
             "P1. Acceptance is an emergent property of a socio-material configuration: interventions that change only the artefact will produce unstable acceptance, and acceptance will vary with water, space, fuel supply and maintenance arrangements, not only with attitudes."),
    "P314": ("P2. End-user agency is distributed",
             "P2. End-user agency is distributed across people, artefacts and institutions: participation mechanisms that address only human voice will fail to shift configurations, and projects that consult residents without changing the material arrangement will not produce durable change."),
    "P315": ("P3. Justice outcomes depend on which actors",
             "P3. Justice outcomes depend on which actors and materials are admitted to the intra-action: the boundary of the project \u2014 who is in the room, what counts as evidence, which infrastructure is treated as fixed \u2014 is a justice decision, and narrow boundaries will produce benefits that are captured by those inside them."),
    "P316": ("P4. A Living Lab is an apparatus",
             "P4. A Living Lab is an apparatus that produces the phenomena it studies: reflexivity about the lab's own cuts is a condition of validity, and a lab that does not document its own interventions will misattribute its findings to the community."),
    "P317": ("P5. Acceptance and justice are related but not identical.",
             "P5. Acceptance and justice are related but not identical: high acceptance can coexist with injustice, as when tenants pay more than owners or women bear additional labour."),
    "P318": ("P6. Participation has material conditions.",
             "P6. Participation has material conditions: the failure of participation is often a failure of design rather than a lack of community interest, and attendance and engagement will vary with the time, place, language and care arrangements of the meeting."),
    "P321": ("These propositions are the bridge to Section 6.",
             "These propositions are the bridge to Section 6. Each component of the conceptual framework is derived from one or more of them, and each is stated in a form that can be examined in the field."),

    # 5.13
    "P323": ("This section has argued that energy interventions in informal settlements",
             "This section has argued that energy interventions in informal settlements fail for reasons that are theoretical as well as technical. The dominant models treat acceptance as an individual decision about a finished artefact, and in doing so they exclude the relations that determine whether a technology works. Critical posthumanism supplies an alternative ontology \u2014 entities emerging through relations, agency distributed, matter generative, knowledge situated \u2014 complemented by energy justice, which supplies normative criteria; systems thinking, which supplies tools for mapping relations and feedback; and Living Lab theory, which supplies a method for organising collaboration."),
    "P324": ("The framework has limits.",
             "The framework has limits: it does not specify distributive criteria, it does not produce policy instruments, and it can be difficult to operationalise. The study manages these limits by treating posthumanism as the foundation rather than the whole, by using energy justice as an operational complement, and by stating its empirical claims in a form that can be examined. The framework yields six propositions about acceptance, agency, boundary, apparatus, justice and participation, and these are the basis for the conceptual framework in Section 6, which turns them into components, relationships and indicators. The distinction between the two sections is deliberate: Section 5 explains why the study thinks the way it does; Section 6 shows what the study will look at."),
    "P325": ("The framework yields six propositions", None),
}

